  "limits": [
    "The six-file Silesia holdout comes from the same overall corpus family as PILOT-007, but these six individual files were not evaluated in earlier POLLICINO pilots.",
    "Only the first 4096 bytes of each holdout file are entropy-coded; full-file identity is recorded in the manifest.",
    "The 0.05 bpb meaningful-gain label and the projected-gain floor are policy choices, not information-theoretic constants.",
    "The neural checkpoint is assumed shared whenever the neural route is available."
  ],
  "conclusions": [
    "The selected stability policy is max_probe=64, recent_window=4, persistence=8, recent_gain_bits=4 and min_projected_gain_bits=0; the value-floor axis did not affect the winning candidate on this development set.",
    "On development it removes all false-positive neural routes but introduces two false negatives (xml and sources-mut), reaching 12/14 route classifications.",
    "On the six-file untouched Silesia subset, stability routing improves categorical route accuracy from 3/6 to 4/6 but worsens mean payload from 3.7139 bpb (PILOT-009 sequential) to 3.7776 bpb.",
    "The reason is asymmetric regret: stability correctly rejects several nearly-neutral neural routes, but misses materially useful neural routes on mozilla (~0.053 bpb gain) and samba (~0.321 bpb gain).",
    "Mean oracle regret rises from 0.0026 bpb for PILOT-009 to 0.0663 bpb for PILOT-010, showing that categorical route accuracy under a fixed 0.05 bpb label is not a sufficient routing objective.",
    "Stability routing sharply reduces neural compute: mean specialist calls fall from 4096 to 727.8 and encode time from 7.09 s to 3.47 s on the holdout runner, while preserving the self-v2 neural route at byte 15.",
    "PILOT-010 is therefore a negative result for hard stability gating as the default compression policy, but a positive result for compute control. The next router objective should be expected regret/value, not classification accuracy."
  ]
}
